  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nHigh pressure will continue to build over the area today and\nlast through Saturday, allowing for dry conditions through the\nfirst half of the weekend. With high pressure overhead and light\nflow across the region, temperatures will dip into the 40s and\nsome upper 30s in sheltered locations but frost potential will\nbe low with highs in the 60s and 70s this afternoon. As high\npressure shifts to the east on Friday and Saturday, southerly\nflow and warm advection into the region will allow for\ntemperatures to get a bump into the 70s and maybe some 80s for\nSaturday with increasing nightly temperatures.\n\nOne minor concern through end of the week will be dry \nconditions during the afternoon hours with relative humidity \nvalues in the 25-40% range for most areas with peak afternoon \nheating and mixing. Given the recent dry spell for much of the \narea, fuel moisture is on the lower end, especially in Northwest\nand Central Ohio. Given this and marginally conducive weather \nconditions, there could be some fire spread concerns if a fire \nwere to develop. However, all conditions with the weather and \nfuels remain too marginal for a headline or even a Special \nWeather Statement.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe main weather concern for the forecast period will be what \nhappens with Hurricane Isaias in the Gulf of America. Isaias \nwill move north and make landfall along the Gulf Coast on Friday\nnight. The main circulation will slow in the Tennessee Valley \nthis weekend, but the moisture from the system will be picked up\nby the flow aloft, allowing for rain to spread north ahead of \nthe center of the circulation. This would allow for rain to \nenter the forecast area after Midnight on Saturday night, \ncontinuing through the day on Sunday. Winds will increase on \nSunday with the advancing remnant low with gusts of 30-40 mph \npossible. Total rainfall will be variable across the forecast \narea with the potential for a sharp gradient of rainfall \ndepending on where the main rainfall axis aligns. Areas of \nNorthwest Ohio may struggle to see a quarter inch of rain or so,\nwhereas southeastern areas could see over an inch and locally\nhigher approaching 1.5-2 inches. Given low river flows and the\nrecent dry pattern, flooding will be localized and nuisance at\nworst over the local area.",
